[[Open This|docker run]]

---

The docker run command creates a new container from a specified image and starts it. It combines docker create and docker start into a single command.

Common options:

`-d` or `--detach`: Run the container in the background (detached mode)).

`-it`: Allocate a pseudo-TTY and keep STDIN open (interactive mode)).<br>
Ex: `docker exec -it uptime-kuma /bin/bash`<br>
[[docker terminal of a container]]

`--name`: Assign a name to the container.

`-p` or `--publish`: Map container ports to host ports (e.g., -p 8080:80).

`-v` or `--volume`: Mount a volume (e.g., -v /host/path:/container/path).

`-e` or `--env`: Set environment variables (e.g., -e MYSQL_ROOT_PASSWORD=mysecret).

`--rm`: Automatically remove the container when it exits (useful for short-lived containers).

`--network`: Connect the container to a specific network (e.g., --network my-network).

!As many containers from an image as you want!

```
docker run -d --name container1 -p 8124:80 nginx

docker run -d --name container2 -p 8125:80 nginx
```

`docker run [OPTIONS] IMAGE [COMMAND] [ARG...]`